= Linear algebra

== Vector spaces
<vector-spaces>
#statement("Definition")[
$C subset.eq V$ is #strong[convex]#index-entry("Convex set") if $t C +\(1 - t\)C subset.eq C$ for $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$. $C$ is #strong[strictly convex]#index-entry("Strictly convex set") if $t C +\(1 - t\)C subset.neq C$ for $0 < t < 1$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$V$ is a vector space of dimension $n$, a $q$ #strong[flag]#index-entry("Flag") is #display-equation($0 = V_0 subset.neq V_1 subset.neq dots.h.c subset.neq V_q = V$) A complete flag is an $n$ flag

]
#statement("Definition")[
$V$ is an $n$ dimensional $bb(F)$-vector, $cal(V) = { v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_n }$ is a basis of $V$, ${ e_1\,dots.h.c\,e_n }$ is the standard basis of $bb(F)^n$, $V arrow.r bb(F)^n\,v_i mapsto e_i$ is an isomorphism, the matrix of $phi.alt in "End"\(V\)$ with respect to $cal(V)$ is #display-equation($A = mat(delim: "[", a_11, dots.h.c, a_(1 n); dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; a_(n 1), dots.h.c, a_(n n))$) Such that $phi.alt\(cal(V)\)= cal(V) A$. If $v = cal(V) c$, then $phi.alt\(v\)= cal(V) A c$

]
== Matrices
<matrices>
#statement("Definition")[
$I\,J subset.eq { 1\,dots.h.c\,n }$, the #emph[submatrix]#index-entry("Submatrix") $A_(I J)$ of $A$ is the matrix with entries ${ a_(i j)\|i in I\,j in J }$. The #emph[principal submatrix]#index-entry("principal submatrix") are matrices $A_(I I)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$E_(i j)$ is the matrix with $1$ on the $\(i\,j\)$-th entry and otherwise zeros, then $E_(i j) E_(k l) = delta_(j k) E_(i l)$

Elementary matrices are single row operations, i.e. #display-equation($e_(i j)\(r\)= mat(delim: "(", 1, , , , ; #none, dots.down, , r, ; #none, , dots.down, , ; #none, , , dots.down, ; #none, , , , 1)$) with $r$ on the $\(i\,j\)$-th entry #display-equation($s_(i j) = mat(delim: "(", 1, , , , , , ; #none, dots.down, , , , , ; #none, , 0, , 1, , ; #none, , , dots.down, , , ; #none, , 1, , 0, , ; #none, , , , , dots.down, ; #none, , , , , , 1)$) and #display-equation($d_i\(r\)= mat(delim: "(", 1, , , , ; #none, dots.down, , , ; #none, , r, , ; #none, , , dots.down, ; #none, , , , 1)$) We have $e_(i j)\(- r\)= e_(i j)\(r\)^(- 1)$ and #display-equation($e_(i j)\(r\)e_(i j)\(s\) & = e_(i j)\(r + s\)\
\[e_(i j)\(r\)\,e_(k l)\(s\)\] & = I + r s delta_(j k) E_(i l) - s r delta_(l i) E_(k j) + delta_(j k) delta_(l i)\(s r s E_(k l) - r s r E_(i j)\)+ r s r s delta_(j k) delta_(l i) E_(i l)\
 & = cases(I quad i eq.not l\,j eq.not k, e_(i l)\(r s\)quad i = l\,j eq.not k, e_(k j)\(- s r\)quad i eq.not l\,j = k, * quad i = l\,j = k)$) Steinberg relations

]
#statement("Definition")[
$E\(n\,R\)subset.eq S L\(n\,G\)$ is the subgroup generated by elementary matrices of determinant $1$. $E\(R\)= union.big E\(n\,R\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$S L\(n\,F\)= E\(n\,F\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$\[E\(n\,R\)\,E\(n\,R\)\]= E\(n\,R\)$ if $n gt.eq 3$

]#label("E(n,R) is perfect")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
For distinct $i\,j\,k$, $e_(i j)\(r\)=\[e_(i k)\(r\)\,e_(k j)\(1\)\]$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Whitehead's lemma])[
$\[G L\(R\)\,G L\(R\)\]= E\(R\)$, hence $K_1\(R\)= G L\(R\)\/E\(R\)$

]#label("Whitehead's lemma")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Since #display-equation($e_12\(1\)e_21\(- 1\)e_12\(1\)= mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; - 1, 0)$) and #display-equation($mat(delim: "(", g, ; #none, g^(- 1)) = mat(delim: "(", 1, g; #none, 1) mat(delim: "(", 1, ; - g^(- 1), 1) mat(delim: "(", 1, g; #none, 1) mat(delim: "(", #none, - 1; 1, )$) We know #display-equation($\[g\,h\]= mat(delim: "(", g, ; #none, g^(- 1)) mat(delim: "(", h, ; #none, h^(- 1)) mat(delim: "(", \(h g\)^(- 1), ; #none, h g) in E\(R\)$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
The #emph[Kronecker product]#index-entry("Kronecker product") of matrices #display-equation($A = mat(delim: "(", a_11, dots.h.c, a_(1 n); dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; a_(m 1), dots.h.c, a_(m n))\,thin B = mat(delim: "(", b_11, dots.h.c, b_(1 p); dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; a_(n p), dots.h.c, b_(n p))$) is #display-equation($A times.o B = mat(delim: "(", a_11 B, dots.h.c, a_(1 n) B; dots.v, dots.down, dots.v; a_(m 1) B, dots.h.c, a_(m n) B)$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
$"tr"\(A^(*) B\)$ defines the #emph[Frobenius inner product]#index-entry("Frobenius inner product") over $M\(n\,bb(C)\)$

]
== Eigenspace decomposition
<eigenspace-decomposition>
#statement("Proposition")[
$T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ is a linear operator, and $V = xor.big_i V_i$, where $V_i$ are $T$ invariant spaces, denote $T\|_(V_i)$ as $T - i$, then $c h_T\(t\)= product_i c h_(T_i)\(t\)$, and $m_T\(t\)= l c m_i thin thin m_(T_i)\(t\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$. $lambda in F$ is an #strong[eigenvalue] if $T v = lambda v$ has nontrivial solution, $v in V$ is a #strong[generalized eigenvector]#index-entry("generalized eigenvector") of rank $m$ of $T$ corresponding to eigenvalue $lambda$ if $\(T - lambda 1_V\)^m v = 0\,\(T - lambda 1_V\)^(m - 1) v eq.not 0$ for some $m gt.eq 1$, and let $V_lambda$ be the subspace of all such generalized eigenvectors, called #strong[generalized eigenspace]#index-entry("Generalized eigenspace"), notice if $V$ is of finite dimensional, then $V_lambda = ker\(T - lambda 1_V\)^m$ for some $m$ with $m$ being smallest, suppose $dim V_lambda = d$, then the characteristic polynomial of $T\|_(V_lambda)$ is $\(t - lambda\)^d$, and the minimal polynomial of $T\|_(V_lambda)$ is $\(t - lambda\)^m$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
$overline(F) = F$, finitely dimensional $F$ vector space $V$ can be decomposed into the direct sum of generalized eigenspaces $V = xor.big_lambda V_lambda$

]#label("Generalized eigenspace decomposition")
#statement("Definition")[
$T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ give $V$ an $F\[x\]$ module with $x dot.op v = T v$, $W lt.eq V$ be a subspace, $W$ is called $T$ #strong[invariant]#index-entry("Invariant subspace") if $T W subset.eq W$, or rather $W$ is an $F\[x\]$ submodule

]
#statement("Definition")[
An linear operator $T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ is called #strong[semisimple]#index-entry("Semisimple linear operator") if $V$ is a semisimple $bb(F)\[x\]$ submodule

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Let $T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ be a linear operator with $overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)$, then $T$ is semisimple $arrow.l.r.double$ $T$ is diagonalizable

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Since $overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)$ and $T$ is semisimple, $V$ can be decomposed as a direct sum of eigenspaces of $T$, thus $T$ is diagonalizable, conversely, if $T$ is diagonalizable, and $T W subset.eq W$, let $V_lambda$ be the eigenspaces of $T$, denote $W_lambda = W inter V_lambda$, and $W' = xor.big_lambda W'_lambda$, since $T\|_(V_lambda) = lambda 1_(V_lambda)$, we can find $W'_lambda lt.eq V_lambda$ such that $V_lambda = W_lambda xor W'_lambda$, and of course $T W'_lambda subset.eq W'_lambda$ which implies $T W' subset.eq W'$, then we have $V = xor.big_lambda V_lambda = xor.big_lambda W_lambda xor W'_lambda = xor.big_lambda W_lambda xor.big xor.big_lambda W'_lambda = W xor W'$

]
#statement("Definition")[
An linear operator $T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ is called nilpotent if $T^k = 0$ for some $k$, $T$ is called unipotent if $T - 1_V$ is nilpotent

]
#statement("Definition", title: [Jordan-Chevalley decomposition])[
#index-entry("Jordan-Chevalley decomposition") #strong[Jordan-Chevalley decomposition] of a linear operator $T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ is $T = T_s + T_n$, where $T_s$ is semisimple, $T_n$ is nilpotent and $\[T_s\,T_n\]= 0$

]#label("Jordan-Chevalley decomposition")
#statement("Theorem")[
If $V$ is a finite dimensional $bb(F)$ vector space with $bb(F)$ being a perfect field, and $T in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ is a linear operator, then Jordan-Chevalley decomposition always exist, additionally, there exist polynomials $p\(t\)\,q\(t\)$ with no constant terms and $T_s = p\(T\)\,T_n = q\(T\)$, moreover, the decomposition is unique

]#label("Existence of Jordan-Chevalley decomposition")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
First consider $overline(bb(F)) = bb(F)$, by Proposition #ref(label("Generalized eigenspace decomposition"), supplement: none), $V$ can be decomposed into the direct sum of generalized eigenspaces $V = xor.big_i V_(lambda_i)$, where $V_(lambda_i) = ker\(T - lambda_i 1_V\)^(m_i)$ with being $m_i$ being the least and $dim V_(lambda_i) = d_i$, define $T_s in upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(V\,V\)$ such that $T_s\|_(V_(lambda_i)) = lambda_i 1_(V_(lambda_i))$ and $T_n = T - T_s$, thus $T_s$ is diagonalizable(semisimple), $T_n$ is nilpotent, $c h_T\(t\)= product_i\(t - lambda_i\)^(d_i)$, by Theorem #ref(label("Chinese remainder theorem"), supplement: none), there exists polynomial $p\(t\)$ such that $p\(t\)equiv 0 #h(0.444em) mod med t$, $p\(t\)equiv lambda_i #h(0.444em) mod med\( t - lambda_i\)^(d_i)$, and let $q\(t\)= t - p\(t\)$, then $p\,q$ doesn't have constant terms and $T_s = p\(T\)\,T_n = q\(T\)$. For uniqueness, suppose $T = T_s + T_n = T'_s + T'_n$ are two such decompositions, then $T_s - T'_s = T'_n - T_n$ will be nilpotent which implies $T_s - T'_s = 0$

]
== Bilinear form
<bilinear-form>
#statement("Definition")[
A #emph[symplectic form]#index-entry("Symplectic form") $omega$ is bilinear form such that $omega\(u\,v\)= X^T J Y$, here $J = mat(delim: "(", #none, - I; I, )$, in other words, there are $u_1\,dots.h.c\,u_n\,v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_n$ such that $omega\(u_i\,v_j\)= - omega\(v_j\,u_i\)= delta_(i j)$, $omega\(u_i\,u_j\)= omega\(v_i\,v_j\)= 0$

]
#aside("Remark")[
$omega\(x xor xi\,y xor eta\)= eta\(x\)- xi\(y\)$ on $V xor V^(*)$ is a symplectic form. Conversely, such a $V$ is called a Lagrangian subspace, a polarization

]
